quic-crypto — provenance note
=============================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE, matching the sibling TLS/DTLS 1.3 crypto
modules `dtls` and `tlsresume` (both self-contained crypto modules with their
own module-local NOTICE, not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

quic-crypto — clean-room implementation from RFC 9001 (Using TLS to Secure
QUIC), §5.1/§5.2/§5.3/§5.4/§6, an open IETF specification. A public
specification is not a copyrightable work (merger doctrine; see
CONVENTIONS.md §5), so no third-party-source NOTICE entry is strictly
required for the RFC citation alone — but the design references consulted are
recorded below for provenance.

`std.crypto` usage (MIT, part of Zig itself): this module reuses TLS 1.3's
HKDF-Expand-Label UNCHANGED via `std.crypto.tls.hkdfExpandLabel` — RFC 9001
§5.1/§5.2 require the TLS 1.3 KDF with its `"tls13 "` label prefix as-is
(the QUIC labels `"quic key"`/`"quic iv"`/`"quic hp"`/`"quic ku"`/
`"client in"`/`"server in"` are supplied as the label, the prefix is not
forked). This is the defining contrast with this repo's `dtls` module, which
targets DTLS 1.3 (RFC 9147 §5.9) and therefore does fork the prefix to
`"dtls13"`. The AEAD primitives (`std.crypto.aead.aes_gcm`,
`chacha_poly`), the single-block AES-ECB (`std.crypto.core.aes`), the
ChaCha20 block keystream (`std.crypto.stream.chacha`), and HKDF/SHA-256 are
used via their public std APIs.

Design reference (studied for construction SHAPE only, NO source copied):
this repo's own `dtls` module (`modules/dtls/src/aead.zig`). RFC 9001 §5.3's
AEAD nonce (`iv XOR left-pad(packet_number)`) and §5.4's header-protection
masks (`AES-ECB(hp, sample)` / raw-ChaCha20 over the sample) are byte-for-byte
the same CONSTRUCTIONS as RFC 9147 §4.2.2/§4.2.3 that `dtls.aead` already
implements. `quic-crypto` re-expresses them QUIC-shaped (variable 1–4-byte
packet-number length, and the long/short-header first-byte low-bit masking
that DTLS has no equivalent of) rather than importing `dtls` — `deps = .{}`,
std-only. No code was copied between the two modules.

Verification (test-time only, neither linked nor vendored): RFC 9001
Appendix A ("Sample Packet Protection", IETF, public) provides the
known-answer vectors carried in the tests. Every Appendix A constant was
cross-checked against two independent oracles before commit — a from-scratch
Python `hmac`/`hashlib` HKDF reimplementation (the §5.1/§5.2/§6 secret
chains) and the `cryptography` package / OpenSSL (the AES-128-GCM /
ChaCha20-Poly1305 seals and the AES-ECB / raw-ChaCha20 header masks); both
reproduced every byte.

**Status: implemented — all crypto bodies are real and validated byte-exact
against the RFC 9001 Appendix A known-answer vectors (both via the
`std.crypto`-direct `sanity` oracles and through the public API), in Debug
and ReleaseFast.**

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
